From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bpereira <bpereira@cloudflare.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 14:22:31 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Drop box state under display:none subtrees and keep scroll
 offsets only on boxes that have them

A subtree that becomes display:none keeps a box tree nothing reads, so release it at damage time
rather than at box construction: a container's construction may skip a display:none child, and then
nothing revisits its subtree. The release drops the layout data except an inner scroller's own
offset, which the document keeps so hiding and showing an ancestor does not move the scroller.

Showing it again needs the opposite care. Box construction records the display for the nodes it
collects as layout children, but a table row, a row group, a flattened inline and a display:contents
element are consumed elsewhere and would keep the `none` that the has-box walks stop at, so the same
block records the current display.

Scroll offsets follow the same rule: a scroll write is applied only to an element that has a layout
box, so nothing clamps an offset against a zero range and writes it back.
